<Base title="About" description="About the author and this site." person>
  <Prose>
    <p class="font-(family-name:--font-ui) uppercase tracking-widest text-(--color-muted) text-xs">About</p>
    <h1>Graham Wright</h1>
    <p>
      I work in nonprofit and cultural organizations, helping teams use technology, creative,
      and communications systems to serve their missions better.
    </p>
    <p>
      I'm currently the Director of Content and Digital Services at the
      <a href="https://www.bso.org">Boston Symphony Orchestra</a>, which also includes the
      Boston Pops, Tanglewood Music Festival, and Symphony Hall. I often describe my team as
      the BSO's internal creative and digital agency. Marketing is our largest focus, but we
      support every part of the institution — artistic, education, fundraising, operations.
      Our work spans digital products and data, storytelling and visual identity, and the
      systems and processes that keep it all running. Innovation and operational excellence
      cut across all of it, and increasingly, that means AI.
    </p>
    <h2>How I got here</h2>
    <p>
      Everything I do now draws on a mix of experiences I didn't plan but wouldn't trade.
      I came to Boston to pursue a PhD in chemistry at MIT and discovered a music community
      that became my home. Over time I left the lab, but not the lab mindset — and built an
      early career as a scientific conference producer while working as a freelance musician.
    </p>
    <p>
      Conference production opened my eyes to a world I hadn't thought about much before.
      In the lab, I'd been focused on research, teaching, and ideas. Producing conferences
      meant understanding how reaching an audience takes marketing, communications, sales,
      and operations.
    </p>
    <p>
      The musician side was its own career. Over more than a decade I performed opera and
      concert repertoire in and around Boston — named roles with Boston Lyric Opera and
      Opera Boston, a solo appearance with the Boston Pops, and performances with ensembles
      including the Handel and Haydn Society and Emmanuel Music. I even sang backup for the
      Rolling Stones. I don't perform much anymore, but the experience is inseparable from
      how I think about this work.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://opusaffair.org">Opus Affair</a> came from both worlds. I founded it
      as a monthly gathering of people interested in the arts — artists, administrators,
      audiences — focused on the social experience around performance, not the performance
      itself. The idea grew out of my life as a musician, and the producer in me knew how to
      build it. The premise was that more people cared about the arts than were visible to one
      another, and that showing up regularly in the right rooms would surface them. It did.
      I'd co-founded the <a href="https://www.bostonsociety.org/">Boston Society</a> years
      earlier, and over time took on interim executive director roles at both that organization
      and <a href="https://www.afarcry.org/">A Far Cry</a> during periods of transition.
      Along the way, I made the decision to grow Opus Affair into a boutique agency doing
      digital and creative work for cultural organizations around the country.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</Base>
